meta {
  name: Shortlisting twice is not an error
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/requisitions/{{jobId}}/shortlist
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "employee_id": "{{strongEmployeeId}}"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
}

tests {
  test("the second POST is accepted, not rejected", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("and it returns the original entry rather than a new one", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().added_at).to.equal(bru.getVar("firstAddedAt"));
  });
}

docs {
  Exactly the same request as the one before it. `add_to_shortlist()` looks for the employee
  first and returns the existing entry if it finds one, so a retry after a dropped
  connection cannot shortlist someone twice.

  The `added_at` comparison is the real assertion — a `201` alone would not prove anything,
  since a duplicate entry would return `201` too. The **timestamp is the identity check**:
  it is unchanged, so this is the original record, not a second one.

  Worth being honest about the trade: this is idempotent, but it answers `201 Created` for a
  request that created nothing. `200 OK` on the duplicate would be more truthful, at the
  cost of a client having to handle two success codes.
}
